			<div class="content" id="post-content-4819652">
									

<p><strong>Seeking Greenlights:</strong> Yes</p>
<p><strong>Page Type:</strong> SCP Article</p>
<p><strong>Genre (Optional):</strong> Horror, Drama/Emotional</p>
<p><strong>Elevator Pitch:</strong> A humanoid SCP who unwillingly jumps between 7 parallel dimensions, in all of which he is in foundation custody. The goal of the containment procedures are to make him believe he is living in a singular reality, by adjusting his cell, keeping personnel up to date on activities, etc. If he discovers a discontinuity, he unconsciously and automatically warps reality to match what he believes to be the baseline. The parallel foundations communicate via a wireless device that he is able to bring with him.</p>
<p><strong>Central Narrative:</strong> A number of incident logs in which he realizes that the world around him has changed, and warps reality to change it back. Symptoms of this involve causing personnel who died in the previous reality to die in the current one and shifts in personality of personnel along the same lines. The first log incident is a member of site staff who died in one of the parallel dimensions talking to the SCP against orders, and being wiped from existence. The second log is an interview in which foundation staff attempt to convince the SCP that he's crazy, so that even if he does notice a change, he might believe it's all in his head rather than a change in reality. The interview continues after the staff leave the interview room, with them debating whether or not what they just did was either morally or tactically correct.</p>
<p>At this point, there will be a denied request to change tactics, and work with the SCP. The third log documents a containment breach that started without warning, after he shifted from a dimension that was experiencing a containment breach. The earlier request is resubmitted and approved. The fourth log is an interview in which the SCP is informed of it's abilities, and is asked to assist in it's own containment, especially in keeping it unaware that he's shifted. He agrees, and the article ends with a pending request to update procedures with suggestions from the SCP.</p>
<p><strong>Hook/Attention-Grabber:</strong> The idea of having to keep an adult humanoid SCP unaware that it's power has activated, especially with coordination between multiple groups with extremely difficult communication. The tension comes from having to sync up quickly, and the consequences of failure. There will also be some exploration of the effects and morality of the foundation having to gaslight him constantly to maintain containment.</p>

							</div>

								
