															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dantheuser" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6984249); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=6984249&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607482248" alt="dantheuser" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=6984249)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/dantheuser" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(6984249); return false;" >dantheuser</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606719084 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">30 Nov 2020 06:51</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4832039">
									

<p><strong>I am a <span style="text-decoration: underline;">newbie</span> and I <span style="text-decoration: underline;">cannot</span> give green-lights!<br />
<em><span style="text-decoration: underline;">Please take my criticism with a LARGE grain of salt</span></em></strong></p>
<p><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">This is a placeholder, I will give my thoughts as soon as I can. If I don't give a review in over 48 hours, please don't be afraid to send me a Wikidot PM. I can easily forget things.</span></p>
<p><em>I'm looking at your idea from a horror story-focused perspective.</em></p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;OPEN&nbsp;CRIT</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;CLOSE&nbsp;CRIT</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<hr />
<p><em>Being too specific on anomalies, not being specific on the important parts</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>A humanoid SCP who unwillingly jumps between 7 parallel dimensions&#8230;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>From my own experience and time of looking up good tips, why do you have to specify exactly how many dimensions the SCP goes through? From what I've read on horror, <em>some of the best things to do is when you keep things vague and mysterious enough that the reader doesn't get the full picture</em>.</p>
<p>You can say that the foundation has so far located 7 parallel dimensions of themselves, but don't make it an exact number. For instance, maybe they've only found 7 so far and aren't sure if any more exist and to what extent. This is just nitpicking, but it's important to give details like these in my opinion.</p>
<hr />
<p><em>Story is not fleshed out; there's some plotholes and questions that arise</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>If he discovers a discontinuity, he unconsciously and automatically warps reality to match what he believes to be the baseline.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>While I do understand the concept, it just doesn't sit right with me because it doesn't feel as fleshed out. The story doesn't feel well-paired with the concept and the SCP alone is quite complex from the beginning.</p>
<p>Things like these need to be explained or answered:</p>
<ul>
<li>How does this happen, is it that is whatever is a discrepancy suddenly changes out of nowhere?</li>
<li>Does the SCP react in any sort of way?</li>
<li>Does the SCP know about its anomaly? You specified that he shouldn't know any discrepancies or else the universe will change, but what is to stop the Foundation from just explaining this to the SCP and having the SCP just be prepared for every switch he makes.</li>
<li>If you DO plan on having it where the foundation can't tell the SCP about his powers, then ask yourself why else would the foundation keep him understudy as an SCP if he isn't supposed to know his powers? You'd think that an SCP who is THIS dangerous and shouldn't know about it powers, would not be held in the way that it is. Maybe give some backstory into the SCP itself and how it manages to preserve this capture as normal?</li>
<li>What stops the Foundation from using simple amnestics on him to help him forget his memory?</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p><em>Give more detail!</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>A number of incident logs in which he realizes that&#8230;<br />
&#8230;procedures with suggestions from the SCP</p>
</blockquote>
<p>See, in-general I feel that this story isn't as detailed and this is a clear example of that.<br />
You say that there will be logs, but nothing is really specified besides some vague actions that occur within them.</p>
<hr />
<p><em>Trying to fix the plothole later on; adding his awareness doesn't solve the issue</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>The fourth log is an interview in which the SCP is informed of it's abilities, and is asked to assist in it's own containment, especially in keeping it unaware that he's shifted.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>See, you can't just fix an issue with the story's beginning by having them tell him later on within the story in this way. It's not like the SCP was just found and the Foundation only recently started testing it, this is after 3 interview logs and god-knows how many tests or studies. I'm sure they would've simply told him this stuff from the begging. This just doesn't fit right here in general and seems like a big plot-hole.</p>
<hr />
<p><em>&quot;Suggestions&quot; aren't given by SCPs</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>He agrees, and the article ends with a pending request to update procedures with suggestions from the SCP.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>and is asked to assist in it's own containment, especially in keeping it unaware that he's shifted.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Remember, your SCP is.. well, an SCP. The Foundation doesn't just accept SCPs' recommendations or suggestions, The Foundation only deals with what helps them in their goals and what the SCP really <span style="text-decoration: underline;">needs</span>. The Foundation may REWARD your SCP for complying, but they don't simply just accept suggestions or ask nicely unless a reason is needed to do so. More of this is explained in the guide <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/so-you-want-to-write-a-humanoid-scp-object">So You Want to Write a Humanoid SCP Object</a> (which I highly suggest you read if you want this to work).</p>
<hr />
<p><em>Improving by simply reading other's work</em></p>
<p>From what I can tell, <strong>although this is just an assumption</strong>, I presume your SCP is inspired by <a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/scp-507">SCP-507</a>? While I do get that maybe you haven't read it, both seem earily similar with a few changes and less depictions. I recommend reading through it, looking at the similarities or how that document did it well, and then figuring out how you can differentiate yourself from it.</p>
<p>I also highly recommend reading some of the most recent SCP articles and seeing what they do right, in order to help flesh out your idea. This way, you can become familiar with the style while also understanding *how* to help out with your goals on this SCP.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong><span style="text-decoration: underline;">BIGGEST PIECE OF ADVICE I CAN GIVE YOU</span></strong></p>
<p><em>Where is the Horror?</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Genre (Optional): Horror&#8230;</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I don't see any real horror going on with this story. So, to allow horror or terror to be given from an article, it's good to keep things vague, mysterious, or thought-provoking. For people to feel horror in your narrative, there has to be something they can relate to and also not-understand.</p>
<p>I HIGHLY recommend looking at some of the most recent articles for this, as the style of horror and SCP articles in-general appears to have changed over the span of years. SCP-096 for instance, although terrifying in its own right, wouldn't be accepted in the same way as if it were to be published today.</p>
<p>Some articles I know that are really useful for understanding this are&#8230;<br />
<a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/things-what-do-a-thing-an-essay-on-things">'Things what do a thing'- An Essay On Anomalies That Are Things That Do A Thing</a><br />
<a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/so-you-want-to-write-a-humanoid-scp-object">So You Want to Write a Humanoid SCP Object</a><br />
<a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/forum/t-521261/horror-writing-tips">Horror writing tips. (Read Hornby's reply on 12 Jul 2012, 19:36)</a><br />
<a href="http://scp-wiki.wikidot.com/mackenzie-tips">Dr. Mackenzie's Documentation Tips</a></p>
<p><em>Give it more story!</em></p>
<blockquote>
<p>Genre (Optional): &#8230;Drama/Emotional</p>
</blockquote>
<p>WHY should I care about discrepancies in the universe?<br />
WHY should I care about multiple versions of the Foundation working together?<br />
WHAT is the SCP's backstory?<br />
WHAT feelings do the Foundation staff show, if any at all?<br />
WHAT are the specifics in the details you're giving us?<br />
HOW is this story something I can really get the sense of &quot;I got a general picture, but there's still a ton I'm unsure about?&quot; (Horror aspect)<br />
WHERE is the emotional connection with the characters?</p>
<p>A good piece of advise would be for you to list out your events, all in order, while specifying how the Foundation, the Staff, the SCP, react to the event you're playing out to them.</p>
<hr />
<p><strong><span style="text-decoration: underline;">FINAL THOUGHTS AND SUMMARY</span></strong></p>
<p><em>TLDR:</em></p>
<ul>
<li>Be more vague on the SCP's anomalies and other factors to increase thought-provoktion and horror</li>
<li>Flesh out your story in-general</li>
<li>Give more detail to parts of your story</li>
<li>Fix the suggestion/recommendtion bits</li>
<li>Improve by reading other authors' work</li>
<li>Give it more horror</li>
<li>Give it more story</li>
</ul>
<p><em>My Overall/Final Thoughts</em></p>
<p>This idea just doesn't seem to work. I'm glad you put so much effort and time into getting critique into this concept, but I just don't feel like it'll work out.</p>
<p>My best advice to you is just scrap the idea and start working on a new idea all together. Use all the critique and advice you had gotten from this one to help you with its creation.</p>
<p>Don't let it wear you down. Not every idea lands, (I learned the hard way around 3 times now), and the best you can do is try to spend more time on making an even better idea than this one.</p>
<p>Good luck to you! Glad I was able to critique this.</p>
<hr /></div>
</div>
</div>

